            <ArticleTitle>Efficacy of serotonin receptor blocker for symptomatic lumbar disc herniation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>159-65</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Serotonin is one of the chemical mediators associated with nerve root inflammation and sciatic symptoms in lumbar disc herniation. The efficacy of serotonin 5-HT(2A) receptor blocker was examined in 44 patients with symptomatic lumbar disc herniation. A selective 5-HT(2A) receptor blocker (sarpogrelate hydroxychloride) was administered orally at a dose of 300 mg per day for 2 weeks. Visual analog scales of low back pain, sciatic pain, and numbness were significantly improved after the administration of the serotonin 5-HT(2A) receptor blocker. Clinical results were good (&gt; 50% pain relief) in 23 patients, fair (25%-50% pain relief) in five patients, and poor (&lt; 25% of pain relief) in 16 patients. Nineteen patients eventually required surgery because of muscle weakness or cauda equina symptoms. The effect of 5-HT(2A) blocker was good in 64% of patients who had uncontained disc herniation, whereas all patients with contained disc herniation had fair or poor results. Patients with uncontained disc herniation responded more favorably to the 5-HT(2A) blocker treatment than patients with contained disc herniation. A 5-HT(2A) blocker has the potential to block the cascade of acute nerve root inflammation and to alleviate symptoms in lumbar disc herniation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
